---
import Architecture from "../components/Architecture.astro";
import Docs from "../layouts/Docs.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.ts";
---

<Docs
  title="How it works"
  description="Your browser tab is the hub of a small peer-to-peer mesh; guests are its members."
>
  <h1>How it works</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Your browser tab is the hub of a small peer-to-peer mesh of its own. Each guest is a member
    of it. There is no PeerShare server that sees your services or your guests' traffic.
  </p>

  <Architecture />

  <h2 id="hub">Your tab is the hub</h2>
  <p>
    When you open PeerShare, your browser creates (or reloads) a key pair and becomes the hub of
    a mesh built with <a href="https://github.com/statewalker/httpeers">httpeers</a>, on
    <a href="https://libp2p.io">libp2p</a>. The hub keeps, in your browser's storage:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>your private key, whose public half is your <strong>peer ID</strong>;</li>
    <li>the list of members: your guests' peer IDs;</li>
    <li>which invites were already used;</li>
    <li>your services: names, addresses, and whether they are on.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    None of this is stored anywhere but your browser. Reloading the page brings the same mesh back, and guests
    reconnect by themselves. Closing the tab stops it: sharing lives exactly as long as your tab.
  </p>

  <h2 id="connections">How two browsers find each other</h2>
  <p>
    A browser cannot accept incoming connections, so both sides first connect to a
    <strong>relay</strong> (<code>relay.httpeers.net</code>) over a secure WebSocket. Your tab
    reserves a slot there; a guest's browser dials your peer ID through it (a libp2p
    <em>circuit relay</em>). Over that circuit the two browsers exchange a WebRTC offer and,
    where the networks allow, switch to a <strong>direct WebRTC connection</strong>; otherwise
    they keep using the circuit.
  </p>
  <p>
    The connection through the relay is secured by a <strong>Noise</strong> handshake between
    the two browsers, so it is encrypted end to end and the relay forwards bytes it cannot read.
    A direct WebRTC connection is encrypted by WebRTC itself (DTLS); its key fingerprints are
    exchanged over that Noise-secured channel, so it is bound to the same two identities. See
    <a href={u("/security")}>Why it is safe</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="joining">Joining with an invite</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Invite</strong> asks your hub for a fresh invitation: a single-use secret that
      expires in 24 hours. The link carries it, with your peer ID and the relay's address, in the
      <em>fragment</em> (after <code>#</code>), which the browser does not send to PeerShare's
      servers. Anyone who can read the message you send it in could use it first.
    </li>
    <li>
      The guest's browser creates its own key pair, connects to your peer ID, and presents the
      invitation. Your hub checks that it is unused and unexpired, marks it spent, and adds the
      guest's peer ID as a member.
    </li>
    <li>
      The hub gives the guest a <strong>membership token</strong>: a
      <a href="https://www.biscuitsec.org">Biscuit</a> signed with your key and bound to the
      guest's peer ID.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="one-request">One request, end to end</h2>
  <p>
    Each service opens for the guest in its own origin, a random name under
    <code>s.httpeers.net</code>. That origin is empty: a ServiceWorker there answers every
    request the service's pages make, including <code>index.html</code>, by handing it to the
    guest's PeerShare page over a <code>MessagePort</code>. The PeerShare page sends it over the
    mesh, with the guest's token, to your tab.
  </p>
  <p>
    Your tab checks the request: the guest's peer ID as proven by the encrypted connection, the
    token's signature and binding, and that the guest is not revoked. Then it fetches the file
    from the service: through the local proxy for a service on your computer or network, or
    directly for a remote https site. The response travels back the same way.
  </p>
  <p>
    Live connections work too: a service's WebSocket (live reload, chat) is carried over the
    mesh as one long request. A service that signs people in with cookies works through the
    local proxy, which keeps each guest's cookies apart.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-runs-where">What runs where</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Piece</th><th>Where</th><th>Role</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>PeerShare page</td><td>Your tab and each guest's tab</td><td>The hub (yours) or a member (theirs)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Relay</td><td><code>relay.httpeers.net</code></td><td>Lets browsers reach each other; forwards encrypted bytes</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Session origins</td><td><code>*.s.httpeers.net</code></td><td>Static files; one isolated origin per service a guest opens</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Local proxy</td><td>Your computer, <code>127.0.0.1</code></td><td>Reaches local services without CORS</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Your services</td><td>Where they already run</td><td>Unchanged: nothing to install in them</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</Docs>
